  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Saturday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: What remains of Hurricane Rachel \ncontinues to bring rain to the Great Basin. Accumulation so far \nhas been mainly limited to White Pine County, Eastern Elko County \nand Northeastern Nye County, but additional development further \nwest is expected this afternoon. Reported totals so far range from\na quarter to a half inch in southeastern Elko to an inch and a \nhalf in the higher elevations of White Pine. By midnight, \ncontinuing rainfall is expected to produce another quarter to half\ninch in western Elko, northwestern Nye, and Lander and Eureka \ncounties, and up to another inch for eastern Elko, northeastern \nNye, and White Pine counties. A flood watch remains in effect for \nmuch of the forecast area until 5 am PDT tomorrow as a result of \nthis continued rainfall. The upper low that absorbed Rachel and \nits moisture will sit atop the Great Basin through Tuesday, \nkeeping showers in place and producing yet another half to three \nquarters inch of precipitation to start the week, some of which \nmay fall as snow as temperatures drop. Most snow will be found at \nhigh elevations, however valleys in eastern Nevada could wake up \nto about a quarter inch of snow on Tuesday. Any valley snow will \nbe short lived as the low exits to the east Tuesday, allowing high\ntemperatures to rise from the low 50s to the upper 60s by \nThursday. Tuesday will also be the final day for precipitation, \nwith dry weather and continued warming expected through the end of\nthe forecast.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence in continuing\nshowers through Tuesday, with moderate confidence in accumulation\ntotals and snowfall potential. Moderate confidence in warming\ntemperatures and dry conditions beyond Tuesday. No changes were\nmade to NBM output.",
